      "heading": "Ohm's law for part of a circuit — resistance",
      "crumb": "Part Two. Foundations of Electrodynamics · Chapter 9. Steady electric current · Printed page 209",
      "captions": [
        "Figure 170",
        "G. S. Ohm (1789-1854) was a German physicist who also taught in a secondary school.He established a current-voltage law for part of a circuit and a law for determining current in a complete circuit.The book's registered correction to his birth year is retained here; the original source wording remains in the source record.For his experiments he overcame substantial difficulties and made a sensitive instrument for measuring current.He used a thermocouple as a supply.Such a device uses two different metals joined at a junction.Its voltage depends on that junction's temperature compared with a reference junction in the measurement circuit.The book describes Ohm increasing this temperature difference to change the voltage.The voltage is approximately proportional to the difference only over a limited range where the voltage change per degree stays nearly constant.Ohm also investigated the dependence of resistance on conductor length and cross-sectional area."
      ],
